# XSH shm_unlink()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# shm_unlink() is SHM (the module's option). Names are "/pc<pid><tag>",
# unique to the test process and removed when it exits.

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.001
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       shm_unlink() of an existing object returns 0, and shm_open()
           of the name without O_CREAT then fails with ENOENT.
test       t/001-name-removed.c

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.002
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.3.2
class      required
since      2001
says       After shm_unlink() of an object that is open and mapped, fstat()
           of the descriptor gives the old size and the mapping shows the
           old data, also after the descriptor is closed.
test       t/002-contents-kept.c

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.003
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       After shm_unlink() of an object that stays open and mapped,
           shm_open(O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_EXCL) of the name makes a new object
           of size 0 whose data is apart from the old one's.
note       The new object, extended by ftruncate(), is checked only not
           to show the old one's byte: no normative text requires an
           extended shared memory object to read as zero (ftruncate()'s
           zero fill is stated for regular files, and shm_open()'s
           RATIONALE is informative).
test       t/003-name-reused.c

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.004
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#ERRORS/ENOENT
class      required
since      2001
says       shm_unlink() of a name with no object fails with ENOENT.
test       t/004-enoent.c

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.005
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#ERRORS/EACCES
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
since      2001
needs      root
says       When shm_unlink() by a process of another user without
           appropriate privileges, of a mode 0600 object, is refused, it
           fails with EACCES and the object keeps its name, size and data.
note       disputed (Q-71): the page never says what permission unlinking
           needs; where it is refused because only the owner may remove
           the name (as with a sticky directory), unlink() allows EPERM or
           EACCES but shm_unlink() lists only EACCES, and XSH 2.3 lets an
           implementation add errors for conditions not described. A
           system that lets the unlink happen is NOTINUSE. Run as root, so
           that the child can take another user ID.
test       t/005-eacces.c

assertion  XSH.shm_unlink.006
anchor     functions/shm_unlink.html#ERRORS/ENAMETOOLONG
class      required
since      2008
says       shm_unlink() of the longest name of the form /pc<pid>zz...z that
           shm_open(O_CREAT) accepted (trying up to 1024 bytes) does not
           fail with ENAMETOOLONG, and returns 0.
test       t/006-no-enametoolong.c
